course: dictionary
lesson: 3691
title: Flashbacks: going back in time
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A flashback is a part of a story, film or play that goes back to show something that happened earlier. Writers use flashbacks to tell you about a character's past, so you understand why they act the way they do.
explain: You can spot a flashback by its clues: the scene jumps back in time, or a character remembers something. Ask yourself, "Is this happening now, or did it happen before?" Then you know if it is a flashback.
explain: Flashback also has a second meaning, about real people. «Flashbacks are scary memories that come back suddenly; if fear does not fade after a scary event, talking to someone helps.» Here a flashback is a sudden, very clear memory, often of something frightening, that can feel as if it is happening again.
fact: A flashback is a part of a story, film or play that shows something from the past.
fact: A flashback can also be a sudden, very clear memory of a past event, often a frightening one.
fact: «Trauma» is the lasting effect of something very frightening, and «flashbacks» are sudden, vivid memories of it.
fact: There is no time limit for getting over something frightening, and counsellors can help.
fact: If fear does not fade after a scary event, talking to someone you trust helps.
exercise: In a film, a flashback is a scene that does what? | *Goes back to show something that happened earlier | Jumps forward to show the ending | Repeats the title song | Shows the actors eating lunch
exercise: «Flashbacks are scary memories that come back suddenly.» What does sudden mean here? | Happening on a Tuesday | *Happening quickly, without warning | Happening only in films | Happening very slowly over many years
exercise: «If fear does not fade after a scary event, talking to someone helps.» What helps when fear does not fade? | Keeping it secret and never mentioning it | *Talking to someone | Watching the same scary film again
test: «The film paused and showed the hero as a small boy, a flashback to the day his home burned.» Where does the scene go? | Nowhere, it stays still | Forward to the next year | Into a different film | *Back to an earlier time
test: A counsellor is someone trained to help people. What can a counsellor do for someone who has flashbacks? | Take the memories away at once with magic | *Help them cope with their memories | Send them to a film | Tell them that the event never happened
exam: «Some people who lived through something very frightening have flashbacks; there is no time limit for getting over it.» What does this tell you? | Scary events are never remembered | *Healing can take as long as it needs, and help is available | Flashbacks only happen to people in films | People must get over every frightening event within one week
